  <iati-identifier>XM-DAC-41302-PMR26-RAS152</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-41302" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International Labour Organization (ILO)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Sustainable enterprises as generators of employment and promoters of innovation and decent work</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In South-East Asia, the area of responsible business conduct or Business and human rights is becoming more critical given the many decent work deficits across the region. The endorsement by the United Nations Human Rights Council of a new set of Guiding Principles for Business and Human Rights in 2011 reinforces the State&#x2019;s duty to protect against human rights abuses by third parties, including business, the corporate responsibility to respect human rights, and greater access by victims to effective remedy, both judicial and non-judicial. 

The ILO and the OECD have evolved international instruments such as the UNGPs and the ILO Tripartite Declaration of Principles concerning Multinational Enterprises and Social Policy (MNE Declaration) and ILO Recommendation 204 concerning the Transition from the Informal to Formal. 

During the current biennium, the ILO supports the promotion of respect for human rights and international labour standards, especially fundamental principles and rights at work, which needs to be promoted as relevant to enterprises of all forms and sizes. In addition, ILO supports the ProSSCE-ASEAN project as an entry point to help solve some of the other labour market challenges as mentioned.

The implementation of responsible business conduct by corporates coupled with improved protection by the State supports micro, small and medium-sized enterprises, and offer protection and respect for workers&#x2019; rights in both the formal and the informal economy.</narrative>
  </description>
